  "question": {
    "text": "If $g(x)=\\int_0^x \\cos 4 t ~d t$, then $g(x+\\pi)$ equals"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "\n$\\frac{g(x)}{g(\\pi)}$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$g(x)+g(\\pi)$\n",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "\n$g(x)-g(\\pi)$\n",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "\nNone of these",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$g(x)=\\int_0^x \\cos 4 t ~d t$\n$\\Rightarrow g^{\\prime}(x)=\\cos 4 x \\quad \\Rightarrow g(x)=\\frac{\\sin 4 x}{4}+k \\quad \\Rightarrow g(x)=\\frac{\\sin 4 x}{4}[\\because g(0)=0]$\n$g(x+\\pi)=g(x)+g(\\pi)=g(x)-g(\\pi)(\\because g(\\pi)=0)$"
  }
}